# 38. Stop conditions

Do not stop an experiment because an external paper was on RGB instead of hyperspectral. That is exactly what our controlled 16-band experiment is meant to verify.

An experiment may stop only for one of these reasons:

1. implementation cannot produce valid finite predictions;
2. GPU/runtime makes the configured model impossible to execute under the available hardware, in which case run the closest smaller official variant and record the exact failure;
3. it violates the organizer's confirmed rule;
4. it is mathematically incompatible with that architecture, in which case run it on the closest compatible architecture as already specified;
5. a prerequisite diagnostic explicitly disproves the premise, for example SAM1 shows no material-pair separability. The diagnostic result itself counts as the completed experiment.

Every stop must still create a ledger record.

